# File Formats, part 1: the text of a column, the markers, the examples.
.h3 The text of a column [appfmt-text]
.ix Text record, bytes of
.ix T record, payload
.ix Tab token
.ix Attribute token
.ix End of line, 1D
.ix Escape 01
.ix Text indent, 1A
.ix No-break space, 1E
.ix Soft hyphen, 1F
The bytes between the T and the NUL of a text record are the text of one
column.  Every column ends with the end-of-line byte 1D, and an empty block is
the single byte 1D.  Several lines in one block are several 1D.
.table 12 58
Bytes | Meaning
20 to FF | A character of code page 437, as it is.
1D | The end of a line: the key {k:Enter}.
01 c | The literal character c.  It is how a picture of the control range is
 | written (the bullet of a list item is 01 07), and how a byte that would
 | be a sign of the format can be a character.
10 f n | A tab.  f is the fill character (usually a space, 20) and n is one less
 | than the number of cells that the tab takes, from the position of
 | the tab to the next stop of the ruler.  The width is worked out again
 | from the stops when the block is edited.
12 m f | A change of attributes.  m is the new mask for all the text that
 | follows (00 normal, 01 bold, 02 underscore words, 04 italic, 08 subscript,
 | 10 superscript, 20 strikethrough, 40 printer specific, and the sums); f
 | is a flag byte: 01 printer specific, 02 underscore all, 04 double underscore
 | words, 08 small caps, 10 double underscore all, 80 a state bit that is
 | kept.  The printer specific ones are kept and print as plain text.
13 t ( ... 13 ) | A marker of type t: see the next section.
1A | The text indent character: lines that follow in the block line up after
 | it, up to the next 1D.
1C | Once at the start of the first block of some documents; kept.
1E | A no-break space.
1F | A soft hyphen: a place where a word may be broken at the end of a line.
 | It is typed with {k:Alt-_}.
other 00 to 1F | Opaque: kept in place, shown as the picture of the code.
.end
.behavior The text of an imported plain file is put in this form by Import
(Reference Manual, chapter 2): a Tab of the file becomes the tab token, a line
end is the byte
1D, and a byte below 20 is written as two characters, the sign {c:^} and a
letter.
.h3 Markers [appfmt-markers]
.ix Marker records
.ix Marker payload
.ix Marker types
.ix Marker field numbers
A marker in a text is written as the byte 13, the type in decimal digits, an
open parenthesis, the payload, and the byte 13 with a close parenthesis.
The payload is a list of records, each ended by CR LF.  A marker may nest:
the payload of a note, a comment or an equation begins with whole S and T
records, which is why a text record is read sequentially.  Every other record
of a payload is one field of the panel of the marker: the first byte is the
number of the field, and the rest is its value (a text ends with NUL, a number
is decimal digits).  A field that has its default value is left out, and the
fields are written in the order of their numbers, which is not always the order
of the panel.
.code
13 "37(" "1F12" 00 CR LF 13 ")"        a Keycap whose legend is F12
13 "9(" S CR LF T "y + 3^x" 1D 00 CR LF 13 ")"     an Equation
13 "5(" 13 ")"                         an Author marker, no payload
.end
The types and their fields are these.  A field number that has
no mark is a text, a mark (n) is a number, and (c) a choice that is stored as a
number.
.table 6 14 50
Type | Marker | Fields
1 | Note | nested text; 1 type (c): left out footnote, 2 levelnote, 3 endnote
2 | Cross-reference | XN section number; XT what is printed: 1 tag (default),
 | | 2 headline, 3 both, 4 page number.
3 | Index | 2 main heading; 3 subheading; 4 reference text; 1 attributes
4 | Keep | 1 (c): left out start fixed, 1 start floating, 2 end
5 | Author | none
6 | Comment | nested text; 1 initials or the name of the author; 2 the time
7 | Date | 1 format (default Month D, Year)
8 | Description | none
9 | Equation | nested text, or 1 file name; 2 width; 3 height; 4 scale;
 | | 5 position (1 floating, 2 in text); 6 name for the table
10 | Worksheet | 1 file name; 4 when to import
11 | Figure | 1 name for the table of figures
12 | Filedate | 1 format
13 | Filename | none
14 | Filetime | 1 format
15 | Font | 1 font number
16 | Include | 1 document name
17 | Counter | 1 counter (1 to 99); 2 start value (0 to 4294967295); 3 style (1
 | | arabic, 2 lower roman, 3 upper roman, 4 upper letters, 5 lower letters)
18 | Line | 1 string; 2 length; 3 weight; 4 center (thousandths)
19 | Merge | 1 variable; 2 delete blank lines (Yes when left out)
20 | Markup | 1 code
21 | Escape Codes | 1 hexadecimal digits
22 | Page Number | none (headers and footers only)
23 | Page Break | 1: 0 page (left out), 1 odd page, 2 even page
24 | Pathname | none
25 | Picture | 1 file name; 2 width; 3 height; 4 scale; 5 position; 6 name
26 | Printer | none
27 | Revision | none
28 | Section | none (headers and footers only)
29 | Table | 1 name for the table of tables
30 | Time | 1 format (default H:MM am/pm)
31 | Whitespace | 1 size (thousandths); 2 treatment (0 crop, 1 next page,
 | | 2 float); U unit (1 cm, 2 mm, 3 pt, 4 bp, 5 pc, 6 dd, 7 cc, 8 sp,
 | | 9 em, 10 ex; none inches)
32 | Symbol | 1 symbol code; 2 symbol name
33 | If | 1 expression
34 | Else | none
35 | Endif | none
36 | Orientation | 1: none the document, 1 portrait, 2 landscape
37 | Keycap | 1 legend; 2 foreground, 3 background (tenths); 4 scale; 5 font
38 | Screen | 1 file name; 2 width; 3 height; 4 scale
39 | Column Break | none
40 | Print Error | 1 message; 2 code; 3 run (made by the Editor after a print)
41 | LaTeX | the nested text (S and T records), no field; the text is the
 | | instructions as typed
.end
A marker of the types 10, 20, 21, 32 and 38 is shown as its label.  Sizes
are in thousandths of an inch (a width of 3 inches is 3000), a scale of 2 is
2000.  The chapter on markers (Reference Manual, chapter 5) says what each
marker means.
.h3 A document, byte by byte [appfmt-example]
.ix Example, new document
.ix Example, saved document
.ix Minimal document
A new unstructured document, as Strata saves it after the text {c:caf} with an
e that has an acute accent (code 82), three spaces, an n with a tilde (code A4),
a space, a c with a cedilla (code 87), a space, a sharp s (code E1) and the word
{c:done} was typed.  Every line is a record; the notation is that of the
conventions above.
.code
*R2                         the release
MR1001                      the revision: one save since a new document
PP\1\ 00                    the page number tag
P1C33                       the title page is centered at 33 per cent
P1P\i\ 00                   the tag of the page number of the title page
P2P\i\ 00                   the same for the table of contents
FHA1                        headlines are bold
FTJ3                        text is justified evenly
FTT5                        tab stops of the text: one, at 5
FSH150 FST150 FSS200 FSC200 spacing
FI3                         indent per level
DP0,8500,11000              paper: letter
YCL99                       kept
YIWN2                       kept
*B0                         the body, not structured
S                           the one section
T caf 82 20 20 20 A4 20 87 20 E1 20 done 1D 00     its text
*E                          the end
.end
Each line in the picture above ends with CR LF in the file; the T record is
one record with the bytes that the line shows, and its end is the byte 1D, the
NUL and CR LF.  The bytes were read from a real file that the Editor wrote.
.h4 A table imported from a text file [appfmt-example-tab]
.ix Example, tab token
.ix Example, imported text
A text file of three lines, each with tabs between three words, imported with
Preserve Line Endings into a new document and saved, has this body.
.code
Item TAB Qty TAB Price
Bolts TAB 12 TAB 3.50
Nuts TAB 100 TAB 0.25
.end
In the file the body of the document is
.code
*B0
S
T Item 10 20 00  Qty 10 20 01  Price 1D
  Bolts 10 20 04  12 10 20 02  3.50 1D
  Nuts 10 20 00  100 10 20 01  0.25 1D
  1D 00
*E
.end
Each tab is the byte 10, the fill character 20 and a width byte, which is the
cache of the width of the tab, as the Editor worked it out when the text came
in (the bytes in the example are those of a real file).  What the screen and a
screen image show is made from the ruler and not from these bytes: with the
default ruler of a new document, which has one stop, at 5, the three columns
of the example are not aligned, and the Export of a screen image writes
{c:Item Qty Price}.  With the stops 12 and 22 in the ruler of the text the
same Export writes the columns at the positions 12 and 22 of each line.  The
last 1D is the empty line that the last line end of the file leaves.
